              block  number,  instead of using the primary superblock (located
              at an offset of 1024 bytes from the beginning of the  file  sys‐
              tem).   If  you specify the _-_s option, you must also provide the
              blocksize of the file system via the _-_b option.    (This  option
              is  rarely  needed; it is used primarily when the file system is
              extremely badly damaged/corrupted.)

       _-_f _c_m_d___f_i_l_e
              Causes ddeebbuuggffss to read in commands from  _c_m_d___f_i_l_e,  and  execute
              them.   When  ddeebbuuggffss  is  finished executing those commands, it
              will exit.

       _-_D     Causes ddeebbuuggffss to open the device using  Direct  I/O,  bypassing
              the  buffer cache.  Note that some Linux devices, notably device
              mapper as of this writing, do not support Direct I/O.

       _-_R _r_e_q_u_e_s_t
              Causes ddeebbuuggffss to execute the single command _r_e_q_u_e_s_t,  and  then
              exit.

       _-_V     print the version number of ddeebbuuggffss and exit.

       --zz _u_n_d_o___f_i_l_e
              Before  overwriting  a file system block, write the old contents
              of the block to an undo file.  This undo file can be  used  with
              e2undo(8)  to restore the old contents of the file system should
              something go wrong.  If  the  empty  string  is  passed  as  the
              undo_file  argument,  the  undo  file  will be written to a file
              named debugfs-_d_e_v_i_c_e.e2undo in the directory specified  via  the
              _E_2_F_S_P_R_O_G_S___U_N_D_O___D_I_R environment variable.

              WARNING: The undo file cannot be used to recover from a power or
              system crash.

